#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186331262 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "624215ff-efdc-5b12-8d61-9d66371672d9" :knowledge-point/title "Determining a Valid Region to Define a Double Integral Over a Non-Rectangular Domain"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9a6d5900-d582-5696-bf23-419f8018557a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331264} {:answer/id #uuid "69ef443a-49a1-5e5f-8a20-4f1e90e21c83" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331265} {:answer/id #uuid "265c5224-c71e-500e-9413-c8bb93873f64" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331266} {:answer/id #uuid "aa93b2dd-d0f6-58e7-b06e-2876a03d82bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331267} {:answer/id #uuid "2c5d92b5-08b3-5380-b46d-c958625a0ec9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331268}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "69ef443a-49a1-5e5f-8a20-4f1e90e21c83" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331265} :answer-field/id #uuid "c82dc270-0c50-53e3-92cb-3ba117c45cfb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331263}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3333941c-b382-595a-913e-d99dd113743a" :question/math-academy-id "q-129666" :question/problem "Let $D$ be a finite region enclosed by the curve $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}=1.$ If \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={\\begin{aligned}x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D\\end{aligned}\\,\\text{and}\\,{∬}_{D}x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nRequire $D\\subseteq R$, so the rectangle contains the entire region. Which of the following could be the definition of the rectangular domain $R?$\n\n- $R=[-2,2]\\times [-3,3]$\n- $R=[-2,2]\\times [-2,2]$\n- $R=[-2,2]\\times [-3,1]$" :question/worked-solution "The ellipse $x^2/4+y^2/9\\le1$ has horizontal extent $[-2,2]$ and vertical extent $[-3,3]$. Rectangle I contains the entire ellipse. Rectangles II and III both exclude the ellipse's point $(0,3)$. Thus only I satisfies the stated containment requirement.\n\nFor a containing rectangle, $f=x\\sin y$ on $D$ and $f=0$ on $R\\setminus D$, so $\\iint_R f\\,dA=\\iint_D x\\sin y\\,dA$. The correct study answer is \"I only\".\n\nThe original unqualified equality alone admits all three rectangles: $D$ and each $R\\cap D$ are symmetric under $x\\mapsto-x$, while $x\\sin y$ changes sign. Every such integral is zero. The explicit requirement $D\\subseteq R$ removes this accidental-cancellation ambiguity."}]}
